for greater diversity

00:14:17.085 --> 00:14:21.059
especially music 'cause
whether you go from classical

00:14:21.059 --> 00:14:24.511
to hip hop, to rap, to pop, whatever.

00:14:24.511 --> 00:14:28.826
There's so much innovation in
music, it's just unbelievable.

00:14:28.826 --> 00:14:32.372
I think actually in the
world of technology and music

00:14:32.372 --> 00:14:34.608
coming together, the level of innovation

00:14:34.608 --> 00:14:36.614
is just accelerating.

00:14:36.614 --> 00:14:40.968
New kind of genre or
music can be invented,

00:14:40.968 --> 00:14:42.243
and you could be behind that.

00:14:42.243 --> 00:14:44.820
So what I learnt, well
I, from Spotify again

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:48.570
is music is at the start
of a amazing journey

00:14:51.423 --> 00:14:53.081
having gone for thousands of years

00:14:53.081 --> 00:14:56.202
but the speed of innovation
and touching audience

00:14:56.202 --> 00:14:58.968
in a way that people
couldn't imagine 10 years ago

00:14:58.968 --> 00:15:01.611
is incredible, and it's all around us.

00:15:01.611 --> 00:15:05.150
I'll really encourage you,
maybe kind of think about

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:07.769
go into those sort of businesses,

00:15:07.769 --> 00:15:10.691
and seeing how they run their business,

00:15:10.691 --> 00:15:12.269
and learn a few things from them

00:15:12.269 --> 00:15:13.903
in terms of what could that mean for you

00:15:13.903 --> 00:15:17.469
that you could mirror and do even better.

00:15:17.469 --> 00:15:19.594
- Yeah I think to have
success in the music industry,

00:15:19.594 --> 00:15:21.008
all right in any business really,

00:15:21.008 --> 00:15:23.738
you need to be able to
inject more view into it.

00:15:23.738 --> 00:15:26.542
As you go out and you learn,
you go to different countries,

00:15:26.542 --> 00:15:27.737
you go to different businesses.

00:15:27.737 --> 00:15:30.360
I saw the Spotify thing on your Instagram.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:33.115
Been following you Stephen,
following the movement around.

00:15:33.115 --> 00:15:36.772
As you go and you discovered
you're making new connections

00:15:36.772 --> 00:15:38.538
or collaborations, go into new countries,

00:15:38.538 --> 00:15:41.681
you learn, you change, and
pour in that into your music.

00:15:41.681 --> 00:15:43.789
We just did a profile
of Machine Gun Kelly,

00:15:43.789 --> 00:15:45.558
who is in hip hop.

00:15:45.558 --> 00:15:48.612
He likes to say he's bringing
in rock and rock to hip hop.

00:15:48.612 --> 00:15:50.125
You can think of these defining artists,

00:15:50.125 --> 00:15:52.089
if you think about Elvis or The Beatles,

00:15:52.089 --> 00:15:54.992
they brought different
influences into their music

00:15:54.992 --> 00:15:56.256
to make it unique.

00:15:56.256 --> 00:15:57.942
If you just sound like somebody else,

00:15:57.942 --> 00:15:59.184
you're never going to really stand out.

00:15:59.184 --> 00:16:01.526
We don't need another
clone of anybody else.

00:16:01.526 --> 00:16:03.867
We need to see what you can bring.

00:16:03.867 --> 00:16:06.925
Even within each of those
artists, you look at The Beatles,

00:16:06.925 --> 00:16:09.254
all of their albums were different.

00:16:09.254 --> 00:16:12.337
As they got more experience,
and they visit other countries

00:16:12.337 --> 00:16:15.565
and met other mentors, they
poured that into their music.

00:16:15.565 --> 00:16:18.919
Their first album sounds
nothing like their last album.

00:16:18.919 --> 00:16:22.211
So figuring out how you
can bring the experiences

00:16:22.211 --> 00:16:23.722
that you had, the connections you had,

00:16:23.722 --> 00:16:25.722
the collaborations you want to make,

00:16:25.722 --> 00:16:27.609
in a new different unique way.

00:16:27.609 --> 00:16:29.387
That's where you're going
to be, it's scary 'cause

00:16:29.387 --> 00:16:31.695
nobody else is doing it but
that's where you're going to

00:16:31.695 --> 00:16:33.816
stand out as we're going
to get the results.

00:16:33.816 --> 00:16:34.898
So that idea--

00:16:34.898 --> 00:16:36.132
- You got to be on the edge, Evan.

00:16:36.132 --> 00:16:37.216
You got to be on the edge, haven't you.

00:16:37.216 --> 00:16:39.188
You got to, the beauty of music,

00:16:39.188 --> 00:16:41.350
you got to constantly reinvent yourself.

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:44.067
Whether you look like a
certain great musician

00:16:44.067 --> 00:16:47.053
who died sadly last year, David Bowie.

00:16:47.053 --> 00:16:49.225
He reinvent himself every decade.

00:16:49.225 --> 00:16:53.201
You talked about The Beatles,
every album's different.

00:16:53.201 --> 00:16:57.368
You look at people like Coldplay,
pretty much the evolution

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:01.693
and them changing their genre of music.

00:17:01.693 --> 00:17:03.511
So think of the next big thing,

00:17:03.511 --> 00:17:07.678
and take their audience with
them is kind of really (faint).

00:17:11.272 --> 00:17:12.874
So on the money then.

00:17:12.874 --> 00:17:14.640
Actually the more we travel,

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:16.883
the more we see different types of music.

00:17:16.883 --> 00:17:18.807
Let different kind of culture's music

00:17:18.807 --> 00:17:21.198
come down and end together
to create something

00:17:21.198 --> 00:17:23.013
really, really unique and special.

00:17:23.013 --> 00:17:26.452
I think if you, again it's
kind of like an entrepreneur

00:17:26.452 --> 00:17:27.364
with an idea.

00:17:27.364 --> 00:17:30.119
If you really believe
in it, you need to go

00:17:30.119 --> 00:17:33.435
and find the audience or the followers.

00:17:33.435 --> 00:17:35.762
Then again, you continue to innovate.

00:17:35.762 --> 00:17:40.091
I think if you just stop where
you are, you become stale.

00:17:40.091 --> 00:17:42.707
It's quite amazing when you
look at the last 20 years

00:17:42.707 --> 00:17:45.782
the kind of guys who
have a one hit wonder.

00:17:45.782 --> 00:17:47.294
They come and they disappeared

00:17:47.294 --> 00:17:49.961
because they failed to innovate.

00:17:51.209 --> 00:17:53.696
- I like it, I like it.

00:17:53.696 --> 00:17:55.973
Free Coupons Magazine is saying amen.

00:17:55.973 --> 00:17:58.094
So I don't know if that
means it's accepting your

00:17:58.094 --> 00:18:01.181
$1 million challenge but amen
with an exclamation point.

00:18:01.181 --> 00:18:02.643
- I love the $1 million challenge.

00:18:02.643 --> 00:18:06.213
Go for it, go for it, we're backing you.

00:18:06.213 --> 00:18:08.530
- Okay cool, guys again, questions in caps

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:09.830
are easier to find.

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:12.893
Next up is Camo, how
do you bring diversity

00:18:12.893 --> 00:18:16.643
without losing its
identity and authenticity?

00:18:18.414 --> 00:18:19.413
- That's a great question.

00:18:19.413 --> 00:18:22.133
I think Camo, what you want to do is

00:18:22.133 --> 00:18:24.800
just think diverse from day one.

00:18:26.191 --> 00:18:28.897
(noise drowns voice)

00:18:28.897 --> 00:18:31.993
There's a book I mentioned
previously kind of blab we did

00:18:31.993 --> 00:18:36.093
by Iris Bohnet, it's a
book called What Works.

00:18:36.093 --> 00:18:38.931
The reason why I kind of
(faint) is there's so many

00:18:38.931 --> 00:18:43.147
kind of subconscious,
unconscious biases we all have.

00:18:43.147 --> 00:18:44.771
I think the first thing you do, Camo,

00:18:44.771 --> 00:18:46.367
is to make sure you talk about it.

00:18:46.367 --> 00:18:50.536
I think the fundamental and I
love that word authenticity.

00:18:50.536 --> 00:18:52.072
'Cause if you talk about stuff,

00:18:52.072 --> 00:18:54.657
you get it out on the
table whatever it is.

00:18:54.657 --> 00:18:57.812
Then you can just passionately say kind of

00:18:57.812 --> 00:19:01.591
why do I think that way or
why were you just recruited

00:19:01.591 --> 00:19:03.540
in that sort of stereotype or why.

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:07.540
How we even place in
the words on our website

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:10.040
or our social media?

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:14.207
'Cause all these little elements
could have joined together

00:19:16.269 --> 00:19:18.880
to actually attract an audience.

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:22.963
Then the other thing I
think really fundamental--

00:19:24.358 --> 00:19:27.254
(noise drowns voice)

00:19:27.254 --> 00:19:28.726
Also transparent.

00:19:28.726 --> 00:19:32.258
Then if you plan diversity
right from the get-go

00:19:32.258 --> 00:19:34.964
into your business, I think
it can be a real competitive

00:19:34.964 --> 00:19:37.354
strength in the long term, and
it builds an awesome culture

00:19:37.354 --> 00:19:39.879
and a culture you'd be really proud of.

00:19:39.879 --> 00:19:43.291
I think the financial returns are better.

00:19:43.291 --> 00:19:45.243
There's tons of data on that.

00:19:45.243 --> 00:19:47.948
But it just makes you proud
that you built something

00:19:47.948 --> 00:19:50.948
very special, very
sustainable, and very diverse,

00:19:50.948 --> 00:19:52.996
and inclusive as well.

00:19:52.996 --> 00:19:55.772
So I think that comes
down to the behaviors.

00:19:55.772 --> 00:19:58.287
There's a whole loads of research as well

00:19:58.287 --> 00:20:01.759
that are around kind of deep bias in.

00:20:01.759 --> 00:20:03.602
'Cause the reality is you
just got to acknowledge

00:20:03.602 --> 00:20:05.514
that we've all got biases.

00:20:05.514 --> 00:20:08.551
We've all got conscious
and sometimes very clearly,

00:20:08.551 --> 00:20:10.634
subconscious preferences.

00:20:12.476 --> 00:20:15.890
The more we actually
challenge them ourselves,

00:20:15.890 --> 00:20:20.094
then the better we'll be as
entrepreneurs and leaders.

00:20:20.094 --> 00:20:23.312
The more actually we feel
complacent and comfortable,

00:20:23.312 --> 00:20:27.479
probably the more we kind
of fester and just regret.

00:20:28.836 --> 00:20:32.455
So I would really say,
Camo, this is what probably

00:20:32.455 --> 00:20:34.210
what Evan and I do kind of most weeks.

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:36.085
We reflect kind of what's gone well,

00:20:36.085 --> 00:20:37.637
what's not gone well.

00:20:37.637 --> 00:20:39.889
What have I done well as a leader?

00:20:39.889 --> 00:20:41.866
What could I done differently?

00:20:41.866 --> 00:20:45.035
Then try to challenge your
sort of subconscious about,

00:20:45.035 --> 00:20:47.254
you know we interviewed these folks,

00:20:47.254 --> 00:20:49.278
and actually it's kind
of great in Sage now.

00:20:49.278 --> 00:20:54.128
I very often go to meetings
where I'm the only guy.

00:20:54.128 --> 00:20:56.967
I think wow, this is cool, I like this.

00:20:56.967 --> 00:21:00.384
Obviously it's way more than just gender.

00:21:01.687 --> 00:21:05.854
Got to get into race, faith,
age, disability, all these areas

00:21:07.525 --> 00:21:10.740
but I think there's some real things

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:13.883
that you as a leader, an
entrepreneur can kind of

00:21:13.883 --> 00:21:15.992
think about, and you're
right to flag up those words.

00:21:15.992 --> 00:21:18.591
Authenticity, I think
it's all about culture.

00:21:18.591 --> 00:21:20.479
It's all about transparency.

00:21:20.479 --> 00:21:23.589
It's all about, I think a
great culture is a company

00:21:23.589 --> 00:21:26.208
where people talk about stuff.

00:21:26.208 --> 00:21:28.987
People don't kind of hide
stuff or stand by their

00:21:28.987 --> 00:21:31.610
coffee machine and just
talk behind your back.

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:34.974
I just think transparency and
openness is a great culture

00:21:34.974 --> 00:21:38.447
'cause ultimately then you
can put teams together,

00:21:38.447 --> 00:21:40.777
squads together, who can work on stuff.

00:21:40.777 --> 00:21:43.313
That means stuff will
change for customers.

00:21:43.313 --> 00:21:44.371
We build value for customers.

00:21:44.371 --> 00:21:48.674
It's all about serving the
guys, who are going to be buying

00:21:48.674 --> 00:21:51.017
your products or your services,

00:21:51.017 --> 00:21:53.040
and really enjoy in what you're building.

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:56.391
Building, taking your
idea and making it real.

00:21:56.391 --> 00:21:58.780
The way you do that is almost as important

00:21:58.780 --> 00:21:59.976
as the idea itself.

00:21:59.976 --> 00:22:04.023
I think you hit on a really
rich vein there actually.

00:22:04.023 --> 00:22:05.356
Camo, thank you.

00:22:06.645 --> 00:22:10.320
- I think if you look in that
diversity, you need to have--

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:13.056
If you're worried about
losing your authenticity,

00:22:13.056 --> 00:22:16.871
you need to know what your mission is.

00:22:16.871 --> 00:22:19.897
You can't have diversity
around the mission.

00:22:19.897 --> 00:22:22.504
This is what our company is here to do.

00:22:22.504 --> 00:22:26.685
Right, like Sage is here
to help entrepreneurs.

00:22:26.685 --> 00:22:29.893
As long as you're surrounded
by that common mission,

00:22:29.893 --> 00:22:32.767
then there's a lot of different
ways to go and do it, right.

00:22:32.767 --> 00:22:36.017
Sage, you guys launched your foundation

00:22:36.998 --> 00:22:40.631
which has a lot of work
around the world helping out.

00:22:40.631 --> 00:22:42.679
Stephen does these to help out,

00:22:42.679 --> 00:22:43.836
and give back to entrepreneurs.

00:22:43.836 --> 00:22:45.822
They have Sage Summit everyday,

00:22:45.822 --> 00:22:49.788
which is a massive party
to help entrepreneurs.

00:22:49.788 --> 00:22:52.323
So it's a lot of different
ways to do it, right.

00:22:52.323 --> 00:22:54.138
There's other ideas that
will come up as new people

00:22:54.138 --> 00:22:56.441
come in to Sage, and as Stephen
comes up with more ideas,

00:22:56.441 --> 00:22:58.502
the way they'll attack will
be a lot of different ways.

00:22:58.502 --> 00:23:00.662
But there's still the
mission of we want to go out

00:23:00.662 --> 00:23:03.400
and help entrepreneurs,
and build the best software

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:04.977
to do it.

00:23:04.977 --> 00:23:07.805
We're not in the car
business or something else.

00:23:07.805 --> 00:23:11.048
So I think as long as you
have a really focused mission

00:23:11.048 --> 00:23:13.437
and you know your why, like
this is what we're trying to do,

00:23:13.437 --> 00:23:15.241
then there's a lot of different
ways to go and attack it

00:23:15.241 --> 00:23:16.408
but not been--

00:23:17.311 --> 00:23:21.466
I think if you have diversity
in the what we're trying to do

00:23:21.466 --> 00:23:22.848
then that could be an issue.

00:23:22.848 --> 00:23:24.555
I think you need to have, as the leader,

00:23:24.555 --> 00:23:25.942
have that clear focus.

00:23:25.942 --> 00:23:28.223
That gives your team the freedom to go off

00:23:28.223 --> 00:23:30.636
and do what they need to do.

00:23:30.636 --> 00:23:32.100
- Yeah, thank you on that.

00:23:32.100 --> 00:23:34.088
I certainly concur.

00:23:34.088 --> 00:23:36.260
You need to be sort of ruthlessly focused

00:23:36.260 --> 00:23:38.370
around your mission, and your idea,

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:40.694
and always stay true to
yourself around that.

00:23:40.694 --> 00:23:43.379
But it's the how that really is important.

00:23:43.379 --> 00:23:44.427
I'll give you an example.

00:23:44.427 --> 00:23:47.318
We're doing loads of
stuff around an area like

00:23:47.318 --> 00:23:50.399
artificial intelligence or
voice so we kind of believe

00:23:50.399 --> 00:23:51.780
entrepreneurship run their business

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:52.950
from the palm of their hand.

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:56.437
They could do that and one
of them could be by text,

00:23:56.437 --> 00:23:59.520
it could be through Facebook Messenger

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:01.386
but it could be through voice.

00:24:01.386 --> 00:24:04.761
So you know, it makes me
sit here and think wow,

00:24:04.761 --> 00:24:07.028
there's so much innovation going on.

00:24:07.028 --> 00:24:09.333
People talked about this is the
fourth industrial revolution

00:24:09.333 --> 00:24:12.674
but if it is, then I think
we've got a responsibility

00:24:12.674 --> 00:24:15.513
as leaders to do it the
right way this time,

00:24:15.513 --> 00:24:17.268
and not kind of reinvent the sins of

00:24:17.268 --> 00:24:19.292
the previous three industrial revolutions.

00:24:19.292 --> 00:24:21.925
So kind of a challenge
we make to ourselves.

00:24:21.925 --> 00:24:25.342
So why say Siri and Alexa a female voice?

00:24:27.021 --> 00:24:29.898
Why do you shout to what is a female voice

00:24:29.898 --> 00:24:32.747
to give instructions to start the car?

00:24:32.747 --> 00:24:34.647
Whereas Goldman Sachs has got

00:24:34.647 --> 00:24:37.670
a big massive investment in AI,

00:24:37.670 --> 00:24:39.948
and they've got Ross the Banker.

00:24:39.948 --> 00:24:43.149
How guys, we just planted
all the old stereotypes

00:24:43.149 --> 00:24:45.904
of the last hundreds of years.

00:24:45.904 --> 00:24:49.695
Why don't we rethink and
reframe the gender question

00:24:49.695 --> 00:24:51.879
and make things gender neutral to give

00:24:51.879 --> 00:24:53.024
the maximum opportunities?

00:24:53.024 --> 00:24:55.278
Now why does it matter to us?

00:24:55.278 --> 00:24:57.656
Because we're doing
loads of apprenticeships

00:24:57.656 --> 00:24:59.593
with school kids.

00:24:59.593 --> 00:25:01.299
We'll invite them in.

00:25:01.299 --> 00:25:04.044
The first time we invited
those school kids in,

00:25:04.044 --> 00:25:07.562
surprise, surprise, 12
candidates came through.

00:25:07.562 --> 00:25:09.906
They were all men, boys.

00:25:09.906 --> 00:25:11.673
They're all 17-year-old boys.

00:25:11.673 --> 00:25:15.040
So we're restricting ourselves
from a massive diverse

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:18.014
talent pool out there if
we just focus on that.

00:25:18.014 --> 00:25:20.167
So the only way you
kind of break this down

00:25:20.167 --> 00:25:21.427
and maybe we can do this,

00:25:21.427 --> 00:25:22.950
it's tougher for an entrepreneur,

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:24.827
but we think we should
go into the schools,

00:25:24.827 --> 00:25:27.168
we should go into the
colleges and excite the girls

00:25:27.168 --> 00:25:30.240
around technology to say,
actually there's nothing

00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:32.077
to fear about this technologies.

00:25:32.077 --> 00:25:36.628
But I think, for us, the reason
why we're digressing a bit

00:25:36.628 --> 00:25:40.687
is it is something we
should, it doesn't challenge

00:25:40.687 --> 00:25:44.224
the AI's going to be for revolutionary,

00:25:44.224 --> 00:25:47.416
voice is going to be a great
technology for the future,

00:25:47.416 --> 00:25:48.989
all these sort of things,

00:25:48.989 --> 00:25:50.948
but it's the how does challenge.

00:25:50.948 --> 00:25:54.572
Why do we propagate the
stereotypes of the past?

00:25:54.572 --> 00:25:59.518
Why can't we rethink and
reframe a gender neutral world?

00:25:59.518 --> 00:26:02.043
And make sure we do it the right way.

00:26:02.043 --> 00:26:04.539
We're talking about ethics
as part of our business.

00:26:04.539 --> 00:26:07.076
This is really fundamental.

00:26:07.076 --> 00:26:09.195
For companies like us.

00:26:09.195 --> 00:26:12.098
It's so easy Evan to
sit there with your idea

00:26:12.098 --> 00:26:14.571
and just do the things you've done before.

00:26:14.571 --> 00:26:17.964
The great leaders not only
stay true to their mission

00:26:17.964 --> 00:26:20.572
as you rightly said but
think about how we frame it

00:26:20.572 --> 00:26:23.460
to make it totally inclusive
for everybody we're serving,

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:25.741
and do it the right way with
the ethics we want to pursue

00:26:25.741 --> 00:26:29.171
in terms of a diverse
and an inclusive world.

00:26:29.171 --> 00:26:30.523
- I love it.

00:26:30.523 --> 00:26:33.326
