It's all available through social,

00:36:18.946 --> 00:36:20.726
and it's all available
through the Internet.

00:36:20.726 --> 00:36:23.944
I would really encourage, and
obviously you're very social

00:36:23.944 --> 00:36:25.748
by coming on this show.

00:36:25.748 --> 00:36:28.942
There's probably loads of
people who are listening now,

00:36:28.942 --> 00:36:32.775
talking pain questions
and who could be really

00:36:33.696 --> 00:36:38.679
powerful when you network to
help your idea come to market?

00:36:38.679 --> 00:36:40.377
- I always love how
Stephen remembers the name

00:36:40.377 --> 00:36:42.888
and uses it in the answer.

00:36:42.888 --> 00:36:45.204
I've been reading the chat
keeping up with people

00:36:45.204 --> 00:36:46.423
and I forgot who asked the question.

00:36:46.423 --> 00:36:49.036
Then Stephen (faint) Kathy.

00:36:49.036 --> 00:36:51.669
He's got that mind there.

00:36:51.669 --> 00:36:54.424
Listen Kathy, I think this
is part of entrepreneurship.

00:36:54.424 --> 00:36:56.253
You never have all the resources you want

00:36:56.253 --> 00:36:57.827
to do the thing that you want to do.

00:36:57.827 --> 00:37:00.190
You got to find another
way to get started.

00:37:00.190 --> 00:37:02.421
Right, don't let the fact
that you don't have the money

00:37:02.421 --> 00:37:04.274
to travel be the reason
why you don't go out

00:37:04.274 --> 00:37:06.152
and try to expand your mind.

00:37:06.152 --> 00:37:09.589
I don't have a building so I can't.

00:37:09.589 --> 00:37:11.408
We're always going to have these reasons

00:37:11.408 --> 00:37:12.895
why we can't do the things.

00:37:12.895 --> 00:37:14.187
Entrepreneurship is about resourcefulness

00:37:14.187 --> 00:37:15.234
and finding a way.

00:37:15.234 --> 00:37:17.987
So what can you do in your
local city to find a way.

00:37:17.987 --> 00:37:20.693
Maybe there's meetup groups.

00:37:20.693 --> 00:37:22.667
There's salsa classes here in Toronto.

00:37:22.667 --> 00:37:25.202
I don't have to go to
different country to learn

00:37:25.202 --> 00:37:26.691
how to dance salsa.

00:37:26.691 --> 00:37:28.819
There's different activities
you can do around this.

00:37:28.819 --> 00:37:30.226
There's different videos you can watch.

00:37:30.226 --> 00:37:33.528
I learn a ton from my own
videos where we cover--

00:37:33.528 --> 00:37:35.869
You guys gave us suggestions
on videos, people to cover.

00:37:35.869 --> 00:37:38.892
People I've never heard of,
who have had massive success

00:37:38.892 --> 00:37:41.320
in countries outside of North America,

00:37:41.320 --> 00:37:43.329
and I get to learn from that.

00:37:43.329 --> 00:37:44.782
So it's just finding a way.

00:37:44.782 --> 00:37:46.001
What do you want to learn?

00:37:46.001 --> 00:37:48.290
What are the skills, what are the people

00:37:48.290 --> 00:37:49.522
you want to be able to connect with?

00:37:49.522 --> 00:37:51.779
There's probably a local
group already covering that

00:37:51.779 --> 00:37:53.106
in some way.

00:37:53.106 --> 00:37:54.799
If not, there's definitely one online

00:37:54.799 --> 00:37:55.910
you can get involved with.

00:37:55.910 --> 00:37:57.995
So much more just about
the resourcefulness

00:37:57.995 --> 00:38:00.245
than the lack of resources.

00:38:01.345 --> 00:38:03.073
People are chiming in where they're from.

00:38:03.073 --> 00:38:04.787
We got Peter from the Netherlands.

00:38:04.787 --> 00:38:08.454
Camo from Paris, Ignatius
from South Africa,

00:38:09.349 --> 00:38:13.177
Alex from Mexico, another
guy named Evan from Canada.

00:38:13.177 --> 00:38:14.725
People from all over the world.

00:38:14.725 --> 00:38:17.419
It's great to see you
tuned in live right now.

00:38:17.419 --> 00:38:18.725
- It's great.

00:38:18.725 --> 00:38:20.686
- Okay, next question, I
got to get to Jonathan.

00:38:20.686 --> 00:38:23.173
Jonathan has asked this question
like 18 times in the chat.

00:38:23.173 --> 00:38:25.333
He really needs an
answer to this question.

00:38:25.333 --> 00:38:27.951
So Jonathan, I appreciate the hustle.

00:38:27.951 --> 00:38:29.852
This question is for you.

00:38:29.852 --> 00:38:33.803
How does one select
people for a diverse team

00:38:33.803 --> 00:38:36.636
which you can trust and grow with?

00:38:37.924 --> 00:38:40.059
- Hey Jonathan, it's a great question.

00:38:40.059 --> 00:38:44.409
I think that is something
there's no easy answer to

00:38:44.409 --> 00:38:48.641
as an entrepreneur 'cause you
know, I made many decisions.

00:38:48.641 --> 00:38:50.553
Some bad decisions over the time.

00:38:50.553 --> 00:38:54.296
I'm sure we all have but
I think the smart way

00:38:54.296 --> 00:38:57.589
to answer that is obviously
I think number one

00:38:57.589 --> 00:39:00.525
is look for the differences
you need to be successful.

00:39:00.525 --> 00:39:01.707
Be quite conscious of that in.

00:39:01.707 --> 00:39:04.957
Unpick any sort deep bias in your mind.

00:39:06.474 --> 00:39:08.557
Then pick the individual.

00:39:09.752 --> 00:39:12.559
The key thing is with all these things,

00:39:12.559 --> 00:39:15.362
is you have to be aligned
around the mission.

00:39:15.362 --> 00:39:18.166
You have to be aligned around the purpose.

00:39:18.166 --> 00:39:20.737
If you want to go into the music business

00:39:20.737 --> 00:39:23.004
and the individual doesn't like music,

00:39:23.004 --> 00:39:24.847
you're going to struggle.

00:39:24.847 --> 00:39:26.881
So they've got to bring something

00:39:26.881 --> 00:39:29.918
but the most important thing
is, you've got the alignment

00:39:29.918 --> 00:39:33.244
and the power of the purpose,
the sense of purpose,

00:39:33.244 --> 00:39:34.707
the sense of passion.

00:39:34.707 --> 00:39:36.999
'Cause you know being
entrepreneur is hard work.

00:39:36.999 --> 00:39:41.166
You do going to have to work
really hard to be successful.

00:39:42.093 --> 00:39:44.923
If they don't have that
far in their belly,

00:39:44.923 --> 00:39:48.228
that you've got about your
big idea, then you might

00:39:48.228 --> 00:39:50.551
as well shut the conversation
down straight away.

00:39:50.551 --> 00:39:53.041
Then once they've got that,
I think then you got to

00:39:53.041 --> 00:39:55.187
ask yourself, what is the
complementary skill set,

00:39:55.187 --> 00:39:56.991
what's the competence,
what's the experience

00:39:56.991 --> 00:39:59.063
that they bring to your success?

00:39:59.063 --> 00:40:01.979
That's very dependent on
where you're on your journey.

00:40:01.979 --> 00:40:06.146
If it's the ideas, probably (faint).

00:40:07.512 --> 00:40:10.840
If it's further down in your
sort of business journey,

00:40:10.840 --> 00:40:14.814
then it could be around sales,
marketing, social media.

00:40:14.814 --> 00:40:16.825
It could be a very good SEO optimization.

00:40:16.825 --> 00:40:19.326
It could be, what is it
you need at that stage?

00:40:19.326 --> 00:40:21.821
I think you should be very
thoughtful about that.

00:40:21.821 --> 00:40:24.748
The next thing is kind
of making that decision.

00:40:24.748 --> 00:40:26.201
Go on with in.

00:40:26.201 --> 00:40:30.541
Then honestly, the best
advice to give you is

00:40:30.541 --> 00:40:34.331
if you make a mistake, make
the mistake to learn early.

00:40:34.331 --> 00:40:36.952
Just as we do in terms of ideas,

00:40:36.952 --> 00:40:39.305
we have a culture where
we want to encourage

00:40:39.305 --> 00:40:41.158
all our colleagues to fail fast.

00:40:41.158 --> 00:40:45.146
Actually, getting into
Spotify, just to a data point.

00:40:45.146 --> 00:40:47.413
They have sort of an idea's factory.

00:40:47.413 --> 00:40:50.607
They expect like nine
out of 10 ideas to fail.

00:40:50.607 --> 00:40:53.849
But one idea will be a kind of absolute

00:40:53.849 --> 00:40:56.555
kind of barnstorm, a ripper.

00:40:56.555 --> 00:40:58.872
They'll be hugely
successful, that one idea.

00:40:58.872 --> 00:41:01.188
But I think within the culture
of a great company again,

00:41:01.188 --> 00:41:02.797
you want to encourage people to fail.

00:41:02.797 --> 00:41:04.943
There's no fear in failure.

00:41:04.943 --> 00:41:08.769
But ultimately if it doesn't
work out as an individual

00:41:08.769 --> 00:41:11.097
down the road, then
it's really important to

00:41:11.097 --> 00:41:13.099
just sit down and be
transparent, authentic

00:41:13.099 --> 00:41:16.609
and just say it's kind of
just not working out with you.

00:41:16.609 --> 00:41:18.828
I made a bad hiring decision.

00:41:18.828 --> 00:41:21.608
I apologize for that but we just got to

00:41:21.608 --> 00:41:23.263
be honest with ourselves.

00:41:23.263 --> 00:41:27.654
The great entrepreneurs,
I think are always

00:41:27.654 --> 00:41:31.067
sort of focused on their
business almost to a level

00:41:31.067 --> 00:41:33.430
of ruthlessness but they're
always very compassionate

00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:37.597
with the people around them,
and very authentic about that.

00:41:39.562 --> 00:41:40.963
- Yeah just, I mean I agree

00:41:40.963 --> 00:41:42.624
with everything Stephen's saying.

00:41:42.624 --> 00:41:44.404
The thought that I'm
going through in my head

00:41:44.404 --> 00:41:47.246
just to add to it is,
I like testing people

00:41:47.246 --> 00:41:48.450
on different projects.

00:41:48.450 --> 00:41:51.206
I think if you can find
an easy way to get tested,

00:41:51.206 --> 00:41:54.243
so give them an assignment.

00:41:54.243 --> 00:41:55.826
So whether you're bringing on interns

00:41:55.826 --> 00:41:57.899
or whether you're in your hiring process,

00:41:57.899 --> 00:42:01.288
another big friend of the
typical job interview process,

00:42:01.288 --> 00:42:03.321
I'll rather just let them
get to work on something

00:42:03.321 --> 00:42:05.531
and see how it goes.

00:42:05.531 --> 00:42:07.541
So I'll give them five
hours to work on a project

00:42:07.541 --> 00:42:10.676
and see what comes up, and
be willing to be surprised,

00:42:10.676 --> 00:42:12.577
and open to new ideas.

00:42:12.577 --> 00:42:16.611
So if you have some kind
of way to test people out

00:42:16.611 --> 00:42:20.306
on a small short term basis,
and again to Stephen's point,

00:42:20.306 --> 00:42:21.355
you're relying on the mission.

00:42:21.355 --> 00:42:23.340
Here's our goals, here's
where we're trying to get to.

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:26.133
But I'm open to how the path,
how we're going to get there.

00:42:26.133 --> 00:42:28.767
If you can find ways to work with people

00:42:28.767 --> 00:42:31.532
and test them out to see
what ideas might come out,

00:42:31.532 --> 00:42:33.091
I think you'll be surprised.

00:42:33.091 --> 00:42:34.789
A lot of the people who are on my team,

00:42:34.789 --> 00:42:37.579
I've a team of misfits, like
they will have a hard time

00:42:37.579 --> 00:42:39.958
getting a job other places.

00:42:39.958 --> 00:42:42.504
They had the worst resumes,
their cover letters were

00:42:42.504 --> 00:42:44.015
you know, wasn't great.

00:42:44.015 --> 00:42:48.182
But I just gave them a
chance, and they surprised me.

00:42:49.111 --> 00:42:52.462
'Cause often the skills, if
you're hiring a video editor,

00:42:52.462 --> 00:42:55.185
their cover letter doesn't
really make a big difference.

00:42:55.185 --> 00:42:56.867
I'm not hiring them for
their writing ability.

00:42:56.867 --> 00:42:59.509
I want to know they can make great videos.

00:42:59.509 --> 00:43:03.676
So to Stephen's point about
not falling in to stereotypes,

00:43:04.923 --> 00:43:08.198
and just giving people a test.

00:43:08.198 --> 00:43:09.824
I think you'll be surprised if you do that

00:43:09.824 --> 00:43:11.408
with enough people.

00:43:11.408 --> 00:43:13.993
- Yeah, I think Evan, the
other thing I love it.

00:43:13.993 --> 00:43:18.391
You're a man the guys I
think, are over all hustlers.

00:43:18.391 --> 00:43:20.026
We love hustlers, right.

00:43:20.026 --> 00:43:23.244
So give him a test,
give him an assignment.

00:43:23.244 --> 00:43:24.648
You always look for people

00:43:24.648 --> 00:43:26.609
who are going to put skin in the game.

00:43:26.609 --> 00:43:29.632
So, I remember when I ran a startup,

00:43:29.632 --> 00:43:32.070
we kind of hardly pay people.

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:37.068
We ran the company on fumes
but we gave some equity out.

00:43:37.068 --> 00:43:41.358
We gave some shares out to
folks who joined early on.

00:43:41.358 --> 00:43:44.104
But boy they have a fantastic work ethic.

00:43:44.104 --> 00:43:46.761
So they proved themselves
on the assignment.

00:43:46.761 --> 00:43:49.624
Then the next stage is,
it's not going to be a land

00:43:49.624 --> 00:43:51.819
of milk and honey in the first month.

00:43:51.819 --> 00:43:53.842
We got to double down here.

00:43:53.842 --> 00:43:55.646
We got to really preserve the cash,

00:43:55.646 --> 00:43:58.681
and make the cash go as far as it can.

00:43:58.681 --> 00:44:00.342
But also, why am I saying that?

00:44:00.342 --> 00:44:03.374
Because it drives the
right ethos of the culture

00:44:03.374 --> 00:44:05.204
in the company.

00:44:05.204 --> 00:44:08.056
It means, you know again, you
kind of treat the company's

00:44:08.056 --> 00:44:10.361
money as if it's your own 'cause it is.

00:44:10.361 --> 00:44:13.190
You make every dollar go as far as it can.

00:44:13.190 --> 00:44:17.056
So 'cause I think when you're
in an early stage company,

00:44:17.056 --> 00:44:18.860
it breaks your heart if you come in

00:44:18.860 --> 00:44:22.663
to see people wasting
money and stuff like that.

00:44:22.663 --> 00:44:25.979
So again, not only do you
test them on assignment

00:44:25.979 --> 00:44:28.027
but you actually test
their cultural values

00:44:28.027 --> 00:44:29.560
and their cultural fit.

00:44:29.560 --> 00:44:31.573
That's a really good thing you can do.

00:44:31.573 --> 00:44:33.732
- Yeah, 100%, 100%.

00:44:33.732 --> 00:44:37.950
Namas is writing in
diverse teams may come with

00:44:37.950 --> 00:44:42.252
creative ideas alongside
when you need quick consensus

00:44:42.252 --> 00:44:44.947
among members with diverse
backgrounds and culture.

00:44:44.947 --> 00:44:47.713
How do we achieve it so
that the firm moves forward

00:44:47.713 --> 00:44:49.213
ahead as one team?

00:44:50.346 --> 00:44:51.248
- That's a great question.

00:44:51.248 --> 00:44:54.498
So hey, Namas, I think, I've got this--

00:44:56.675 --> 00:44:58.124
Language is pretty important to me

00:44:58.124 --> 00:44:59.649
so I think it's really good.

00:44:59.649 --> 00:45:02.551
The more diverse you
have, you tend to find

00:45:02.551 --> 00:45:03.705
different cultures coming together

00:45:03.705 --> 00:45:06.608
so I think you need a
common language around

00:45:06.608 --> 00:45:07.912
how do we serve in?

00:45:07.912 --> 00:45:10.182
Number one, always use phrases like

00:45:10.182 --> 00:45:12.620
we got to put ourselves in
the shoes of the customer.

00:45:12.620 --> 00:45:14.302
What does a customer need?

00:45:14.302 --> 00:45:17.813
So even in meetings, I
sometimes keep an empty chair

00:45:17.813 --> 00:45:20.419
for the customer, and say
what would the customer want?

00:45:20.419 --> 00:45:22.724
What would the customer
want us to do here?

00:45:22.724 --> 00:45:25.799
Then I've always encourage
to have a culture

00:45:25.799 --> 00:45:27.896
where you get facts and data in the room.

00:45:27.896 --> 00:45:31.515
If anybody's got a pointed
view, they should share.

00:45:31.515 --> 00:45:34.759
I think as a leader, you
want to encourage the people

00:45:34.759 --> 00:45:36.503
who don't speak often.

00:45:36.503 --> 00:45:39.135
'Cause sometimes they're the
brightest people in the room

00:45:39.135 --> 00:45:40.832
but they might feel intimidated.

00:45:40.832 --> 00:45:43.709
So and then when you've got that,

00:45:43.709 --> 00:45:45.781
you frame what is it we're
trying to achieve here.

00:45:45.781 --> 00:45:47.927
What is the right answer for the customer?

00:45:47.927 --> 00:45:49.035
How do we get to market?

00:45:49.035 --> 00:45:52.218
Whatever the problem of the day is,

00:45:52.218 --> 00:45:57.129
and then you get agreement
around what the decision is.

00:45:57.129 --> 00:45:59.947
Then a phrase I use internally a lot is

00:45:59.947 --> 00:46:03.943
if you make a decision, and
you take no accountability,

00:46:03.943 --> 00:46:06.112
no action, and no timeline,

00:46:06.112 --> 00:46:09.466
and no outcome of what that decision is,

00:46:09.466 --> 00:46:11.988
then if you leave the
scene of that decision,

00:46:11.988 --> 00:46:15.648
then consider there is
no decision ever made.

00:46:15.648 --> 00:46:17.402
Because a decision without an outcome

00:46:17.402 --> 00:46:19.902
or an action is hallucination.

00:46:21.653 --> 00:46:23.141
It never happens.

00:46:23.141 --> 00:46:26.337
So we saw, I think a great
culture, a great culture

00:46:26.337 --> 00:46:28.351
is where you have the discussion,

00:46:28.351 --> 00:46:29.667
you get the facts and the data in,

00:46:29.667 --> 00:46:31.834
and you put yourself in
the shoes of the customer

00:46:31.834 --> 00:46:33.377
saying how do we make a difference here.

00:46:33.377 --> 00:46:35.086
You make a decision, you lock that down.

00:46:35.086 --> 00:46:37.207
Get everybody crystal clear about

00:46:37.207 --> 00:46:38.816
what their accountability is,

00:46:38.816 --> 00:46:40.791
and who's going to be responsible for

00:46:40.791 --> 00:46:43.131
delivering that for the team.

00:46:43.131 --> 00:46:44.253
It could be a team effort.

00:46:44.253 --> 00:46:46.803
Then you say right, what
does success look like?

00:46:46.803 --> 00:46:47.935
What's the outcome?

00:46:47.935 --> 00:46:48.768
When are we going to have it?

00:46:48.768 --> 00:46:50.801
30 days, 10 days, five days?

00:46:50.801 --> 00:46:53.020
And say, let's get back
together the next week,

00:46:53.020 --> 00:46:56.065
let's have a coffee, and just make sure

00:46:56.065 --> 00:46:57.603
we did what we said we would.

00:46:57.603 --> 00:47:01.905
So I think as a leader, you can
really be a bit of a hustler

00:47:01.905 --> 00:47:04.820
and also I think where this is important,

00:47:04.820 --> 00:47:06.548
you got to be authentic
again with yourself.

00:47:06.548 --> 00:47:08.355
You got to hold yourself to accounts.

00:47:08.355 --> 00:47:10.377
So if you commit to things for the team,

00:47:10.377 --> 00:47:13.085
you've got to deliver them
for, and you've got to

00:47:13.085 --> 00:47:14.360
walk the talk and set the tone

00:47:14.360 --> 00:47:17.068
for the company you're building.

00:47:17.068 --> 00:47:19.373
- I like that empty seat for the customer.

00:47:19.373 --> 00:47:21.326
I've never heard, I've
consumed a lot of content,

00:47:21.326 --> 00:47:22.838
I've never heard anybody do that,

00:47:22.838 --> 00:47:25.276
have an empty seat and
imagine the customer's there.

00:47:25.276 --> 00:47:27.776
That's cool, I might use that.

00:47:29.553 --> 00:47:31.347
I think, two things, I agree with Stephen.

00:47:31.347 --> 00:47:35.296
I think the mission is the unifying goal,

00:47:35.296 --> 00:47:36.491
like why we're doing this.

00:47:36.491 --> 00:47:39.563
If you have clarity on why you're here

00:47:39.563 --> 00:47:40.963
that makes it so much
easier to gather around

00:47:40.963 --> 00:47:43.171
and move forward.

00:47:43.171 --> 00:47:45.610
I think, two in terms
of cultural diversity,

00:47:45.610 --> 00:47:49.730
a lot of the work can be
done in advance as well.

00:47:49.730 --> 00:47:52.349
Ideally, if you're over communicate it

00:47:52.349 --> 00:47:53.875
and really try to understand each other,

00:47:53.875 --> 00:47:56.532
then you already have a
sense of how to work together

00:47:56.532 --> 00:47:58.107
before you come to the table

00:47:58.107 --> 00:47:59.702
to make this really important decision,

00:47:59.702 --> 00:48:02.518
and not have to figure
that out on the spot.

00:48:02.518 --> 00:48:05.114
If you look at Stephen,
he led off with a quote

00:48:05.114 --> 00:48:07.943
